            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\": \"The Holy Spirit, eternal life, and the spiritually satisfying, life-giving grace of God mediated through Jesus Christ.\",\"primary_basis\": [\"John 4:10-14\", \"John 7:37-39\", \"Jeremiah 2:13\", \"Revelation 7:17\", \"Revelation 21:6\", \"Revelation 22:1-17\"],\"alternatives\": [{\"meaning\": \"The sacrament of Christian baptism, historically associated with fresh, flowing water\",\"basis\": [\"John 3:5\", \"Ephesians 5:26\", \"Hebrews 10:22\"],\"prominence\": \"common\"},{\"meaning\": \"The Word of God or the Gospel message that purifies and sustains the believer\",\"basis\": [\"Ephesians 5:26\", \"John 15:3\"],\"prominence\": \"minority\"},{\"meaning\": \"Divine wisdom or the Torah, primarily emphasizing the Jewish intertestamental context\",\"basis\": [\"Proverbs 13:14\", \"Proverbs 18:4\"],\"prominence\": \"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\": true,\"confidence\": \"high\",\"notes\": \"In its original ancient Near Eastern and linguistic context, 'living water' literally referred to flowing spring water as opposed to stagnant well or cistern water. Christian consensus is heavily anchored by John 7:39, which explicitly identifies 'living water' with the Holy Spirit. While the primary symbolic consensus universally centers on the Spirit and eternal life, liturgical and patristic traditions frequently extend the metaphor to baptismal regeneration.\"}",
